'''Prem Singh Yadav''' is an Indian scientist who led the ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes (ICAR-CIRB) team that produced the cloned Murrah bull Hisar Gaurav in 2015, making CIRB India's second institute to produce a cloned buffalo<ref name="src-443a3f8b23">{{cite web|url=https://icar.gov.in/node/4887|title=ICAR-CIRB become India’s second center to produce cloned buffalo|website=icar.gov.in|date=11 December 2015}}</ref>. Independent reporting later identified Yadav as head of CIRB's buffalo-cloning team<ref name="src-d03b0f5819">{{cite web|url=https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/india/cibr-scientists-clone-7-elite-m-29-buffaloes-re-clone-one-5561561.html|title=CIBR scientists clone 7 elite M-29 buffaloes, re-clone one|website=Moneycontrol|date=17 July 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220929222324/https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/india/cibr-scientists-clone-7-elite-m-29-buffaloes-re-clone-one-5561561.html|archive-date=29 September 2022}}</ref>.

In 2015, a team led by Yadav produced Hisar Gaurav, a cloned [[Murrah buffalo|Murrah]] bull, making ICAR-CIRB India's second institute to produce a cloned buffalo, after the National Dairy Research Institute<ref name="src-443a3f8b23">{{cite web|url=https://icar.gov.in/node/4887|title=ICAR-CIRB become India’s second center to produce cloned buffalo|website=icar.gov.in|date=2016}}</ref><ref name="src-hindu">{{cite web|url=https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/other-states/Hisar-institute-becomes-second-centre-to-clone-buffalo/article13993135.ece|title=Hisar institute becomes second centre to clone buffalo|website=The Hindu|date=11 January 2016}}</ref>. His team produced India's first cloned Assamese buffalo, Sach-Gaurav, in 2017<ref name="src-b4ab04664d">{{cite web|url=https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/indias-first-cloned-assamese-buffalo-born-1824454|title=India’s First Cloned Assamese Buffalo Born|website=NDTV|date=15 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190811220112/https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/indias-first-cloned-assamese-buffalo-born-1824454|archive-date=11 August 2019}}</ref>, and in 2020 produced seven clones of the elite breeding bull M-29 and a re-clone of Hisar Gaurav, reported by ICAR as the world's first multiple clones and re-clone of a Murrah buffalo<ref name="src-edb32eef83">{{cite web|url=https://www.amarujala.com/haryana/hisar/cirb-hisar-has-created-seven-cloned-pieces-one-re-clone-hisar-news-rtk5643533104|title=सीआईआरबी हिसार ने तैयार किए सात क्लोन कटड़े, एक री-क्लोन भी बनाया|website=Amar Ujala|date=17 July 2020}}</ref><ref name="src-9a6bdc8d41">{{cite web|url=https://icar.org.in/en/node/12458|title=ICAR-CIRB makes commendable achievement in Buffalo Cloning by producing 7 clones from a Single Breeding Bull|website=Indian Council of Agricultural Research|date=16 July 2020}}</ref><ref name="src-6ee08c1bb8">{{cite web|url=https://zeenews.india.com/india/cibr-hisar-achieves-a-milestone-clones-8-calves-to-improve-buffalo-breed-2296320.html|title=CIBR Hisar achieves a milestone, clones 8 calves to improve buffalo breeding|website=Zee News|date=2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200717125628/https://zeenews.india.com/india/cibr-hisar-achieves-a-milestone-clones-8-calves-to-improve-buffalo-breed-2296320.html|archive-date=17 July 2020}}</ref>.

Yadav received the Fellowship of the Society of Animal Physiologists of India in 2016<ref name="src-62ed1b164c">{{cite web|url=https://cirb.res.in/news/icar-cirb-scientists-awarded/|title=ICAR-CIRB Scientists Awarded - ICAR - Central Institute|website=cirb.res.in|date=20 January 2018}}</ref>, the Distinguished Scientist Award of the Indian Society for Buffalo Development in 2019<ref name="src-d3e79cefed">{{cite web|url=https://cirb.res.in/news/national-conference-of-the-society/|title=Distinguished Scientist Award to Dr PS Yadav, ICAR-CIRB Hisar|website=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|date=2019}}</ref>, and led the team that received the 2019 [[Indian Council of Agricultural Research|ICAR]] Nanaji Deshmukh Award for Outstanding Interdisciplinary Team Research<ref name="src-4deaa5ee4f">{{cite web|url=https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/2022-08/ICAR_Citations_final_2020_Low_0.pdf|title=ICAR Award 2019 Citations|website=Indian Council of Agricultural Research|date=2020}}</ref>. He retired from ICAR-CIRB as a principal scientist in 2025<ref name="src-30cdb84a23" />.

==Career==
Yadav joined the Indian Council of Agricultural Research as a scientist on 12 April 1993<ref name="src-e73557644a">{{cite web|url=https://cirb.res.in/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Staff-Remuneration-List-Hisar-2023.pdf|title=Scientists Remunerations|website=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|date=2023}}</ref>. In 2003, while a senior scientist, he began a 12-month Department of Biotechnology overseas associateship in [[Neustadt, Hesse|Neustadt]], Germany<ref name="src-75cd616383">{{cite web|url=https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/inline-files/PARTNERSHIP-AND-LINKAGES-02_0.pdf|title=ICAR Partnership and Linkages: Overseas training during 2003–04|website=Indian Council of Agricultural Research|date=2003}}</ref>. He undertook a research stay at the Institute for Farm Animal Genetics in Germany from 28 December 2010 to 10 February 2011<ref name="src-4fdcf94f36">{{cite web|url=https://cirb.res.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/annualreport2010-2011.pdf|title=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes Annual Report 2010–11|website=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|date=2010–11}}</ref>, and later served as head of ICAR-CIRB's Division of Animal Physiology and Reproduction<ref name="src-87ac8ccc96">{{cite web|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210723192859/https://cirb.icar.gov.in/ps-yadav/|title=Dr. Prem Singh Yadav|website=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes (archived official profile)|date=23 July 2021}}</ref>.

==Research==
Yadav led the ICAR-CIRB project that used semen from Hisar Gaurav to produce 20 calves through artificial insemination by 2018<ref name="src-12f91675f5">{{cite web|url=https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/archive/haryana/cloned-bull-s-semen-used-to-produce-20-calves-in-8-months-698521/|title=Cloned bull's semen used to produce 20 calves in 8 months|website=The Tribune|date=14 December 2018}}</ref>. Field trials proved the fertility of the cloned bull's semen and its viability for commercial breeding through artificial insemination<ref name="src-72b5acbb17">{{cite web|url=https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/archive/haryana/cloned-murrah-bull-semen-successfully-used-for-ai-dr-prem-singh-yadav-589214|title=The Tribune India (Hisar Edition): Cloned Murrah Bull Hisar Gaurav Semen Successfully Used for AI under Dr. Prem Singh Yadav|website=The Tribune India (Hisar / Haryana)|date=13 March 2019}}</ref>, and Yadav developed protocols for artificial insemination and high-quality frozen semen production from the cloned bull<ref name="src-d018579193">{{cite web|url=https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/archive/hisar/hisar-gaurav-buffalo-semen-production-prem-singh-yadav-178492|title=The Tribune: Hisar Gaurav Cloned Male Murrah Bull High Quality Semen Production under Dr. Prem Singh Yadav|website=The Tribune (India)|date=17 May 2019}}</ref>. By 2021, ICAR-CIRB had produced ten cloned bulls under the cloning project headed by Yadav, aimed at improving breed and milk production<ref name="src-b96db52eb3">{{cite web|url=https://www.bhaskar.com/local/haryana/hisar/news/central-buffalo-research-institute-of-hisar-to-take-up-cowherds-of-up-punjab-and-rajasthan-to-increase-the-income-of-farmers-128124846.html|title=bhaskar.com|website=bhaskar.com|date=14 January 2021}}</ref>. By 2022, about 14,000 semen doses had been prepared from Hisar Gaurav and distributed free to farmers in villages of Nuh district<ref name="src-a26d426313">{{cite web|url=https://www.etvbharat.com/english/bharat/hisar-gaurav-first-cloned-calf-born-at-icar-cirb-turns-7/na20221211201520284284768|title='Hisar Gaurav', first cloned calf born at ICAR-CIRB, turns 7|website=ETV Bharat|date=11 December 2022}}</ref>. Research led by Yadav found that female progeny of Hisar Gaurav produced 300–600 litres more milk per lactation<ref name="src-e4007d0265">{{cite web|url=https://www.kisantak.in/animal-husbandry/story/indias-first-clone-pt-bull-hisar-gaurav-in-cirb-increase-milking-and-re-production-nsr-1135812-2024-12-11|title=PT Bull: भैंसों का विक्की डोनर है ये बुल, रिसर्च में इस्तेमाल होता है सीमेन, पढ़ें इसकी खूबियां|website=Kisan Tak|date=11 December 2024}}</ref>.

In December 2022, ICAR reported that Yadav was among the CIRB team members who produced Veer Gaurav, a buffalo calf cloned from Hisar Gaurav's semen using ovum pick-up and in-vitro embryo production technology<ref name="src-veer-gaurav">{{cite web|url=https://icar.org.in/en/node/17212|title=ICAR-CIRB produces Veer Gaurav through OPU-IVEP technology|website=Indian Council of Agricultural Research|date=25 December 2022}}</ref>.

==Awards and recognition==
In December 2016, Yadav received the Fellowship of the Society of Animal Physiologists of India, which the institute describes as awarded annually to one leading animal physiologist of the country<ref name="src-62ed1b164c" />. In 2019, he led the Animal and Fisheries Sciences team that received the Nanaji Deshmukh ICAR Award for Outstanding Interdisciplinary Team Research; ICAR recorded 12 eligible applications for the two 2019 categories<ref name="src-4deaa5ee4f" />. The Indian Society for Buffalo Development conferred its Distinguished Scientist Award on Yadav at its national conference in January 2019<ref name="src-d3e79cefed" />. At the Global Summit on Innovations in Reproductive Biology and 36th annual meeting of the Indian Society for the Study of Reproduction and Fertility in Varanasi, held 20–22 February 2026, Yadav delivered the Prof. S. S. Guraya Memorial Oration, titled “Technological advances in buffalo cloning: from embryo optimization to field-level validation”<ref name="src-guraya-2026">{{cite web|url=https://www.bhu.ac.in/Images/files/Abstract%20book%20GSIRB-ISSRF%202026%20BHU%281%29.pdf|title=GSIRB-ISSRF 2026 abstract book|website=Banaras Hindu University|date=2026}}</ref><ref name="src-issrf-2026">{{cite web|url=https://www.issrf.org/conferences/past-conferences|title=Past conferences|website=Indian Society for the Study of Reproduction and Fertility|date=2026}}</ref>.

==Selected publications==
* Yadav was first author of a 2024 Theriogenology study evaluating growth, health, telomere length and semen characteristics in cloned and re-cloned buffalo bulls<ref name="src-f264a3ee95">{{cite web|url=https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37793222/|title=Evaluation of postnatal growth, hematology, telomere length and semen attributes of multiple clones and re-clone of superior buffalo breeding bulls|website=PubMed|date=2024}}</ref>.
* Yadav co-authored a 2020 [[PLOS One|PLOS ONE]] study of the semen characteristics and fertility of a cloned water buffalo bull<ref name="src-a15f669816">{{cite web|url=https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0237766|title=Semen parameters and fertility potency of a cloned water buffalo bull produced from a semen-derived epithelial cell|website=PLOS ONE|date=21 August 2020}}</ref>.
* Yadav co-designed the research and was a corresponding author of the 2019 [[Scientific Reports]] paper “Successful cloning of a superior buffalo bull”<ref name="src-9c0f3d0c0c">{{cite web|url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-019-47909-8|title=Successful cloning of a superior buffalo bull|website=Scientific Reports|date=6 August 2019}}</ref>.
* Yadav co-authored a 2019 peer-reviewed review chapter on telomerase structure, regulation and measurement<ref name="src-8d2b29b28d">{{cite web|url=https://doi.org/10.5772/intechopen.89506|title=Telomerase Structure and Function, Activity and Its Regulation with Emerging Methods of Measurement in Eukaryotes|website=doi.org|date=24 December 2019}}</ref>.
* Yadav co-authored the book “Reproductive Biotechnology in Buffalo” (ISBN 8189304801)<ref name="src-11b51d3d09">{{cite web|url=https://ndri.res.in/sites/default/files/2025-06/ASAP%20April-2025.pdf|title=List of Indian e-Books of ASAP Publication (2018-20)|website=ICAR-National Dairy Research Institute|date=April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251028060629/https://ndri.res.in/sites/default/files/2025-06/ASAP%20April-2025.pdf|archive-date=28 October 2025}}</ref>.
* Yadav was first author of the 2005 paper “Bovine ICM derived cells express the Oct4 ortholog” in Molecular Reproduction and Development<ref name="src-0c979bd135">{{cite web|url=https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15973686/|title=Bovine ICM derived cells express the Oct4 ortholog|website=PubMed|date=2005}}</ref>.

